We have a directed graph with N vertices, numbered 1, 2, \ldots, N. Information about the edges is given by N^2 characters C_{1, 1}, C_{1, 2}, \ldots, C_{1, N}, C_{2, 1}, \ldots, C_{N, N}. Here, each C_{i, j} is either a lowercase English letter or -. If C_{i, j} is a lowercase English letter, then there is exactly one directed edge from vertex i to vertex j labeled C_{i, j}. If C_{i, j} is -, there is no edge from vertex i to vertex j. Note that self-loops (edges from a vertex to itself) are allowed and are considered in the problem. For each integer pair (i, j) with 1 \leq i, j \leq N, answer the following question:
- Among all (not necessarily simple) paths from vertex i to vertex j whose concatenation of labels on the edges forms a palindrome, what is the length of the shortest such path? 
Input
The input is given from Standard Input in the following format:
N
C_{1, 1}C_{1, 2}\ldotsC_{1, N}
C_{2, 1}C_{2, 2}\ldotsC_{2, N}
\vdots
C_{N, 1}C_{N, 2}\ldotsC_{N, N}
Output
Let A_{i, j} be the answer to the question for the pair (i, j). Print them in the following format:
A_{1, 1} A_{1, 2} \ldots A_{1, N}
A_{2, 1} A_{2, 2} \ldots A_{2, N}
\vdots
A_{N, 1} A_{N, 2} \ldots A_{N, N}
Constraints
- 1 \leq N \leq 100
- N is an integer.
- Each C_{i, j} is either a lowercase English letter or -.

